verdict=IN-SCOPE: the approved plan's section 1 names this as step one — VERSION 2.59 to 2.60 in the release's own final commit, because the aims make this a capability release. One line, self-judged.
# Self-judged, and said so rather than implied. The diff is one line.
# 
# `VERSION` 2.59 -> 2.60, amended into `d4ab294`, this release's own final commit.
# 
# The approved plan's section 1 names exactly this as its first step, with the
# reason: the aims add a tool, so this is a capability release, every capability
# release that ships to main bumps the middle number, and the VERSION edit rides
# that release's own final commit so the commit reads as the new base in the
# changelog. Shipped history is never renumbered, so it is now or it is wrong
# permanently.
# 
# It rides the final commit rather than standing alone deliberately. A VERSION-only
# commit is filtered out of the ⓘ dialog since d33ab04 — which is correct, because
# "this release is 2.59" told the reader a number printed above it — but the
# versioning rule wants the release commit to READ as the new base in the
# changelog, and only a commit that reaches the reader can do that. Amending it
# into the black-screen fix satisfies both: that note now reads v2.60, and no
# empty line is added.
# 
# Nothing else is in this diff.
